.TH CHTTPSVR_ROUTER_USE 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_router_use \- append middleware to a sub-router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_router_use(chttpsvr_router *router,
                                   chttpsvr_middleware_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends a middleware to the chain of the sub-router that you give.
This middleware runs after the global middleware, and before the route
handler.  It runs only for a request that the server routes through
this sub-router.

It also runs for a request that the server routes to this sub-router
and then rejects with 413 before its handler could run, and for the
404 or the 405 of a path that this sub-router owns and that none of
its routes matches.  See chttpsvr_subrouter(3) for which paths a
sub-router owns, and chttpsvr_use(3) for the full rule and for the
rejections that run no middleware at all.

This function is thread-safe and you can call it at any time.  You can
also call it while another thread runs chttpsvr_destroy on the owning
server.  A call that races such a destroy returns ccol_invalid_args,
and touches no freed memory.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The NOTES section of chttpsvr_use describes three more things: the cap
that each router applies at registration time, the combined limit of
32 middleware steps for each request at dispatch time, and the policy
that the first of two duplicate routes wins.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when router or fn is NULL, or when something
                  destroyed the server that owns router.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
ccol_not_permitted when the middleware chain of this router is already
at the cap of 32 entries.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_use (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3)
